<BlogPostLayout title="Create Barcode Images: UPC, EAN, Code 128 & QR | BarcodeNest" heading="How to create barcode images in SVG or PNG" description="Choose UPC-A, EAN-13, Code 128 or QR, create an SVG or PNG, and prepare it for printing. Learn what a generator does and when you need an assigned GTIN." author="BarcodeNest Editorial Team" published="2026-11-24" readingTime="5 min read">
<div class="article-shell container"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#format">Choose a format</a><a href="#create">Create an image</a><a href="#print">Prepare for printing</a><a href="#batch">Generate batches</a></aside><div class="article-body">
<p>To create a barcode image, choose the symbol type, provide the value you want it to encode and download the result. The right type depends on how the barcode will be used: a retail product identifier, an internal inventory SKU and a website URL serve different workflows.</p>
<p>The <a href="/barcode-generator/">BarcodeNest barcode generator</a> creates EAN-13, UPC-A, Code 128 and QR images in SVG or PNG after signup. It draws the encoded symbol; it does not assign ownership of a product number or register a product with retailers.</p>
<h2 id="format">Which barcode format should you choose?</h2><ul><li><strong>EAN-13:</strong> encode a complete 13-digit retail identifier, including its check digit.</li><li><strong>UPC-A:</strong> encode a complete 12-digit retail identifier, including its check digit.</li><li><strong>Code 128:</strong> encode internal identifiers such as <code>SKU-002</code> when your receiving system supports that symbol.</li><li><strong>QR:</strong> encode text or a URL. A plain QR containing a URL is not automatically a GS1 Digital Link implementation.</li></ul>
<p>For retail products, use the assigned identifier for that specific product and packaging level. Do not invent a UPC or copy another product's number because it passes a check-digit test. GS1 explains the identifier and symbol workflow in its <a href="https://www.gs1.org/standards/barcodes/10-steps-to-barcode-your-product/english">steps to barcode your product</a>. Internal inventory users can read our <a href="/blog/sku-vs-upc-inventory/">SKU vs UPC guide</a> before choosing an identifier scheme.</p>
<h2 id="create">Create a barcode online in four steps</h2><ol><li>Sign in and open the generator.</li><li>Select EAN-13, UPC-A, Code 128 or QR.</li><li>Enter the value exactly, preserving leading zeros. For UPC and EAN, include the existing check digit.</li><li>Choose SVG or PNG, generate the image and download it.</li></ol>
<p>Free accounts include 100 generated images per month. Each new generation uses one unit. Downloading an existing result again reuses that file. If a UPC or EAN is rejected, compare every digit with the original source; the <a href="/barcode-validator/">barcode validator</a> can help identify a checksum mismatch.</p>
<h2>SVG vs. PNG for a barcode</h2><p>SVG is a vector format useful in layouts and design workflows that preserve vector geometry. PNG is a raster image useful when a destination requires pixels. BarcodeNest PNG output includes 300-DPI metadata, but the final printed size still depends on your layout and printer settings.</p>
<p>A higher DPI label alone does not guarantee a readable print. Scaling a small raster image, stretching its width independently of its height, or allowing a document editor to resample it can change the bars. Keep the intended dimensions and inspect the exported document before printing.</p>
<h2 id="print">Keep margins and test the physical output</h2><p>Preserve the clear area around the barcode, commonly called the quiet zone. Keep nearby text, borders and graphics outside it. Use a high-contrast design, avoid reflective or wrinkled label placement, and test with the scanner that will read the finished package.</p>
<p>GS1's <a href="https://ref.gs1.org/guidelines/2d-in-retail">retail barcode implementation guidance</a> distinguishes decoding from print-quality verification. Reading an exported image with the <a href="/barcode-scanner/">image scanner</a> is useful for checking its content. It does not certify the final printed label or replace verification required by a trading partner.</p>
<h2 id="batch">Generate multiple barcodes or call the API</h2><p>Paid ZIP generation accepts one value per line using one selected symbol type and output format. Creator includes 1,000 images per month and up to 25 per ZIP request. Starter supports 2,000 images and 25 per request; Growth supports 10,000 images and 100 per request. Each image in a ZIP consumes one generation unit.</p>
<p>For an integration, send a request to the same rendering API using an API key. This example generates an internal SKU as an SVG:</p><CodeBlock label="Generate a Code 128 SVG" language="bash" code={request} />
<p>See the <a href="/docs/barcode-tools/">generation and batch API reference</a> for the supported payloads. To place product names and repeated copies on a PDF sheet, use the <a href="/barcode-labels/">label printing tool</a> instead. Generation, scanning and printed labels have separate account allowances, shown on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.</p><p><a class="button button-signal" href="/barcode-generator/">Create a barcode image</a></p>
